# Router

The router decides which handler answers a request. You rarely call it
yourself: you describe routes on controllers, mount the controllers on your
app, and Howdy matches every incoming method and path against them.

This page explains how to declare routes, the rules the router follows when
more than one route could match, and what it answers when nothing does.

## Declaring routes

A controller groups routes under a path prefix. Each route is a method, a
path pattern and a handler:

```gleam
import howdy/controller.{type Context}

pub fn user_controller() {
  controller.new("users")
  |> controller.get("/", list_users)
  |> controller.get("/:id", show_user)
  |> controller.post("/", create_user)
  |> controller.delete("/:id", delete_user)
}

fn list_users(ctx: Context) {
  controller.text(ctx, "Everyone")
}
```

There is a helper for each common method: `controller.get`, `post`, `put`,
`patch` and `delete`. For any other method, use `controller.route` and pass
the method yourself:

```gleam
import gleam/http

controller.new("users")
|> controller.route(http.Other("PURGE"), "/cache", purge_cache)
```

The prefix and the route path are joined to make the full pattern, so the
user controller answers `/users` and `/users/:id`. Use `controller.new("")`
for routes at the root of your site, and a prefix such as `"api/users"` to
mount under more than one segment.

Mount controllers on the app to make their routes live:

```gleam
import howdy

pub fn app() {
  howdy.new()
  |> howdy.controller(user_controller())
  |> howdy.controller(order_controller())
}
```

### HEAD requests

You do not need to declare `HEAD` routes. A `HEAD` request is answered by the
matching `GET` route: your handler runs as usual, and the server sends its
status and headers without the body. That keeps `curl -I`, uptime monitors
and link checkers working on every page with no extra code.

Your `GET` handler does all of its work for a `HEAD` request, even though
the body is thrown away. If that work is expensive, declare a `HEAD` route of
your own with `controller.route(http.Head, ...)`. An explicit `HEAD` route
always takes priority over the `GET` one.

## Path patterns

A pattern is split on `/` into segments, and the request path is split the
same way. Empty segments are dropped from both, so leading, trailing and
doubled slashes never matter: `"users"`, `"/users"` and `"/users/"` are the
same prefix, and a request for `/users/42/` matches `/users/:id`.

There are three kinds of segment:

- `users` is a literal. It matches exactly that text, as in `/users`.
- `:name` is a parameter. It matches any one segment and captures it as
  `name`, so `/users/:id` matches `/users/42`.
- `*name` is a wildcard. It matches the rest of the path and captures it as
  `name`, so `/files/*path` matches `/files/a/b.txt`. It must be the last
  segment of the pattern.

Literal segments are compared exactly, so matching is case sensitive.

A `:name` segment always needs a value: `/users/:id` does not match `/users`.
A `*name` segment matches even when nothing is left, so `/files/*path`
matches `/files` too, with `path` set to an empty string. When it captures
several segments they are joined with `/`, giving `a/b.txt` in the example
above.

## Reading parameters

Captured values are strings. `controller.param` looks one up and returns a
`Result`:

```gleam
fn show_file(ctx: Context) {
  case controller.param(ctx, "path") {
    Ok(path) -> controller.text(ctx, "You asked for " <> path)
    Error(Nil) -> controller.status(ctx, 404)
  }
}
```

Most of the time the `howdy/param` module is more convenient. Its functions
work with `use`, hand you a typed value, and answer `400` for you when the
parameter is missing or has the wrong shape:

```gleam
import gleam/int
import howdy/param

fn show_user(ctx: Context) {
  use id <- param.int(ctx, "id")
  // `id` is an Int here. A request for /users/abc never gets this far.
  controller.text(ctx, "User " <> int.to_string(id))
}
```

Use `param.string` when any text will do. [Path parameters](/docs/controllers/path-parameters)
has the details.

## Which route wins

When several routes could match a request, the router picks one by two
rules, in this order:

1. *Exact routes beat wildcard routes.* A route whose pattern ends in `*name`
   is only considered when no other route matches the path.
2. *Otherwise, the first one declared wins.* Controllers are checked in the
   order they were mounted, and routes within a controller in the order they
   were added.

The second rule means a parameter can shadow a literal declared after it:

```gleam
controller.new("users")
|> controller.get("/:id", show_user)
|> controller.get("/all", list_users)
```

Here `/users/all` reaches `show_user` with `id` set to `"all"`, because
`/:id` was declared first and matches any single segment. Declare the more
specific route first:

```gleam
controller.new("users")
|> controller.get("/all", list_users)
|> controller.get("/:id", show_user)
```

The first rule is what makes a catch-all safe to use. A static file
controller mounted at `/*path` will never take a request away from one of
your own routes, wherever it is mounted. Order only matters between two
wildcard routes, so mount your most general catch-all last.

## When nothing matches

The router gives one of three results, and Howdy turns each into a response:

- `Found`: a route matches the path and the method. The response is
  whatever your handler returns.
- `MethodNotAllowed`: routes match the path, but none with this method. The
  response is `405` with an `allow` header listing the methods that do.
  `HEAD` is listed alongside `GET`, since every `GET` route answers both.
- `NotFound`: no route matches the path. The response is `404` with an empty
  body, unless the app has a [`not_found` handler](#Your-own-404).

So with the user controller from the top of this page, `PUT /users/42`
answers `405` with `allow: GET, HEAD, DELETE`, and `GET /orders` answers `404`.

Exact routes take precedence here as well. If an exact route matches the
path, the wildcards are not tried, even when the exact route has the wrong
method. With `POST /files/upload` and `GET /files/*path` both declared,
`GET /files/upload` answers `405`, not the wildcard.

Middleware is part of the matched handler, so it does not run for `405`
responses, nor for `404` ones unless the app has a `not_found` handler.

### Your own 404

`howdy.not_found` answers every request that no route matches, whatever its
method:

```gleam
howdy.new()
|> howdy.controller(user_controller())
|> howdy.not_found(fn(ctx) {
  service.error_response(ctx, service.NotFound("no such page"))
})
```

The handler runs inside the app's middleware like any route, so request
logging, CORS and your own middleware see these requests too. It gets the
request with no path parameters; with a [version group](/docs/middleware/versioning),
`ctx.version` is the version the request named, if any. A path that routes
match with another method still answers `405`.

A catch-all route matches before it: an app that mounts
`static.serve("/", from: "priv/public")` sends every unmatched `GET` to the
file server, whose own `404` is the controller's, and reaches `not_found` only
with other methods.

### OPTIONS requests

You do not need to declare `OPTIONS` routes. When an `OPTIONS` request
reaches a path that has routes, the router answers `204` with an `allow`
header listing their methods. That response runs inside the controller's
middleware, so CORS middleware sees preflight requests, but the controller's
guard is skipped, because browsers send preflights without credentials. An
`OPTIONS` route you declare yourself always takes priority.

## How matching works

Routes are compiled into a table once, when the app's handler is built, not
on every request. App and controller middleware are wrapped around each
handler at the same moment.

Exact routes are indexed by how many segments they have and by their first
literal segment. A request for `/users/42` is only compared against routes
with two segments that start with `users` or with a parameter, however many
routes your app has. Declaration order is preserved inside that index, so
the rules above hold exactly.

## Trying the router directly

`router.match` runs the same matching on a list of controllers, without a
server. It is handy in tests when you want to check your routes rather than
your handlers:

```gleam
import gleam/dict
import gleam/http
import howdy/router

pub fn user_routes_test() {
  let controllers = [user_controller()]

  let assert router.Found(params:, ..) =
    router.match(controllers, http.Get, "/users/42")
  assert dict.get(params, "id") == Ok("42")

  let assert router.MethodNotAllowed(allowed: [http.Get, http.Head, http.Delete]) =
    router.match(controllers, http.Put, "/users/42")

  let assert router.NotFound = router.match(controllers, http.Get, "/orders")
}
```

To test whole requests and responses instead, send them through your app
with `howdy/testing`.
